niger president mohammad isafu votes  not to stay in power but to elect a  successor  he chose to step aside at the end of his  second term  at the time some west african leaders  tweaked their country's constitution  to remain in office the significance of  this day  is not lost on the 69 year old president  today is a special day for us it is also  and most of all a special day for nijaya  which will experience for the first time  in its history a democratic changeover  and i hope that this change will be the  first step towards other changes in  order to solidify our democratic process  i hope this first changeover will enable  niger to solidify its reputation as a  democratic country  in africa and in the world and this is  the man he designated as successor  former minister of interior and foreign  affairs  bazoo muhammad is challenged by 29 other  candidates  among them two former heads of state and  a former prime minister  hussein adamu says he's voted in every  election since independence  and is here again to choose a leader for  niger  every time i do this as a duty to my  country but also expecting our leaders  will fix our problems  but for samira abubakar it's the thrill  of voting for the first time  i can tell you it's exciting it's like  no other i like casting my ballot  the elections were held amid safety  concerns after an uptick in violence  killed more than 50 people this month  among them members of nigeria's armed  forces  many voters list security and poverty as  their main concerns  now attacks by armed groups near the  border with nigeria mali and burkina  faso have killed hundreds of soldiers  and civilians in recent years  the local economy has also been hit hard  by covet 19  a fall in the prices of its main export  uranium  as well as a long-term closure of the  nigerian border  that many essential goods are imported  across  officials held the elections as a  success for the most part  there are a few places where the  elections could not be held but in many  others we were able to organize voting  despite security threats  results of the elections are expected to  be announced within five days  and ratified by the constitutional court  in two weeks  a winner will need fifty percent of the  vote plus one  to avoid a runoff otherwise the top two  candidates  will head to a second round in february  next year  ahmed al jazeera near me
 

